                <pb facs="0319" n="235" sameAs="frus:frus1886/pg235"/>

                <head>No. 94.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Reinberg to Mr. Hunter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Guayaquil, Ecuador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-02-24"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 24, 1885</date>.
                        (Received March 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 104.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: About 10 o’clock a.m. of the 19th instant
                    Captain Mahan, commanding U. S. S. Wachusett, arrived at Santa Helena, on the
                    coast of Ecuador, on his return from interviewing the prisoner Julio Romano
                    Santos.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>By today’s steamer from the south I received from Captain Mahan original
                    declarations (copies of which I inclose), and shall forward same to Hon. Horatio
                    N. Beach, consul-general, now at Quito, by first mail.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>MARTIN REINBERG,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Vice-Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>




